---

<Base
  title="R3 gap map: ELO/IMM substrate liberation — Surya"
  description="The evidence map for epitaxial lift-off and inverted metamorphic III-V: what is physically demonstrated, what the reuse-economics literature actually shows, and where the gap to constellation-scale economics sits. Sweep executed August 2026."
  path="/r3-gap-map/"
>
  <style>
    article { padding: var(--space-8) 0 0; }
    h1 { font-size: var(--text-2xl); letter-spacing: -0.01em; margin: 0 0 var(--space-3); }
    h2 {
      font-size: var(--text-xl);
      margin: var(--space-8) 0 var(--space-3);
      padding-top: var(--space-5);
      border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    }
    .lede { color: var(--fg-dim); font-size: var(--text-lg); max-width: 46rem; }
    p, li { max-width: 46rem; }
    ul, ol { padding-left: var(--space-5); }
    li { margin-bottom: var(--space-2); }
    .class { font-family: ui-monospace, monospace; font-size: var(--text-sm); color: var(--accent); }
    .finding {
      border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-dim);
      padding-left: var(--space-4);
      color: var(--fg-dim);
    }
    .finding b { color: var(--accent); }
    table.nums {
      width: 100%;
      border-collapse: collapse;
      font-size: var(--text-sm);
      margin: var(--space-4) 0;
    }
    table.nums th, table.nums td {
      text-align: left;
      padding: var(--space-2) var(--space-3);
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
      vertical-align: top;
    }
    table.nums th { color: var(--fg-dim); font-weight: 600; }
    table.nums td:first-child { font-weight: 600; }
    .srcs { font-size: var(--text-sm); color: var(--fg-dim); }
    .srcs li { margin-bottom: var(--space-1); }
  </style>

  <article>
    <div class="wrap">
      <h1>R3: substrate liberation, mapped</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Sweep executed 18 August 2026. The finding: ELO/IMM is physically
        demonstrated — 13 µm epitaxial stacks at ~31% AM0 and &gt;3,000 W/kg,
        film-laminated flight heritage since 2009 — but the economic thesis
        is undemonstrated on every leg. The binding constraint is per-cycle
        reconditioning cost and process throughput, not reuse cycle count;
        and the "~10 reuse cycles" threshold in the originating article
        traces to a modeling assumption, not an experiment.
      </p>

      <h2>Why this map exists</h2>
      <p>
        R3 is the only direction that attacks the germanium chokepoint while
        keeping III-V performance: lift the junction off the wafer, reuse the
        wafer as a template. The originating article poses three sub-questions
        — ELO throughput and yield at industrial scale, substrate reuse cycle
        count (~10 cycles as the order-of-magnitude threshold), and IMM
        mechanical reliability without the handle wafer — and sets the win
        condition: reuse economics close at constellation volumes. Four
        parallel sweeps (throughput, reuse, mechanical, player claims) mapped
        what is actually on the record.
      </p>

      <h2>Throughput: hours per wafer, and no yield data anywhere</h2>
      <table class="nums">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Process</th><th>Best published evidence</th><th>Implied per-wafer time</th><th>Class</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {throughput.map(([p, ev, t, c]) => (
            <tr><td>{p}</td><td>{ev}</td><td>{t}</td><td>{c}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        <span class="class">established</span> The scale gap is derivable:
        10,000 m²/yr of constellation demand is ≈565,000 six-inch wafer
        lift-offs per year, ≈1,550 per day. At the fastest published etch
        rates that is on the order of 160–260 etch stations running
        continuously — before yield losses. No public evidence shows any ELO
        facility at even 1% of that throughput, and no source anywhere
        publishes ELO process yield. Alta Devices ran an ELO pilot/production
        line and failed commercially (closed 2019; equipment auctioned).
      </p>

      <h2>Reuse: the "~10 cycles" threshold is an assumption</h2>
      <table class="nums">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Substrate / route</th><th>Demonstrated</th><th>Caveat</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {reuse.map(([r, d, c]) => (
            <tr><td>{r}</td><td>{d}</td><td>{c}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="finding">
        <b>The correction:</b> the originating article's "~10 reuse cycles is
        the threshold where germanium exposure drops by an order of
        magnitude" is arithmetically true but experimentally unsupported. The
        figure circulates in LCA and cost literature as an assumption — one
        peer-reviewed LCA states "5 to 10 wafer reuses have already been
        achieved" citing Adams 2013 and Lee 2012, whose own demonstrated
        counts are lower or unstated — and NREL's cost models
        <em>assume</em> 10–100 reuses to show competitiveness. The strongest
        demonstrated counts are 4 (GaAs, 1997, materials metrics only) and 1
        (Ge, 2023). Ge reuse after classic AlAs-sacrificial ELO is
        undemonstrated entirely; the demonstrated Ge routes are porous-Ge and
        spalling, one cycle each.
      </p>
      <p>
        <span class="class">established</span> And the cycle count is not the
        binding variable anyway. NREL's bottom-up techno-economic analysis
        (Ward 2016) of all three substrate-liberation strategies — ELO,
        spalling, porous-Ge — found cost reduction is limited "not by the
        number of reuse cycles achievable, but by the costs incurred in each
        cycle to prepare the substrate for another epitaxial deposition":
        CMP reconditioning at an estimated 10–40 µm and $9–34 per cell per
        cycle, against a substrate that costs &gt;$30/W. Protection-layer
        schemes that eliminate CMP exist in the literature (Michigan, IBM,
        Houston) but have no published per-cycle device-performance series.
      </p>

      <h2>Mechanical: what holds the cell together</h2>
      <p>
        <span class="class">established</span> Two demonstrated answers:
        metal carrier stacks (ISE/tf2's IMM4J uses the back-contact metal as
        the mechanical carrier — 13 µm total stack, 140 g/m², 30.9% AM0;
        Radboud transfers to ~20 µm Au or ~25 µm Cu) and polymer/silicone
        laminates (JAXA's Space Solar Sheet: IMM-3J strings in silicone
        between films, &lt;0.2 mm total, ~600 W/kg at sheet level, &gt;150
        W/kg at paddle level, internal qualification tests passed within
        ±2.5% with no crack propagation). The ELO peel-bend itself does not
        damage devices (Radboud accelerated ageing). What does damage them:
        handling of unsupported films (cracks and shunts documented in the
        ISE IMM4J work) and copper carriers under thermal stress (&gt;60%
        efficiency loss after 37 days at 200 °C from Cu diffusion, vs ~10%
        for gold).
      </p>
      <p>
        <span class="class">gap</span> No published AIAA S-111/S-112 or
        ECSS-E-ST-20-08C qualification campaign exists for any substrate-free
        thin-film III-V cell or laminate — qualified metamorphic products
        (SolAero Z4J, IMM-β) retain or do not disclose their substrate. No
        cyclic-flexure dataset (bend radius vs cycles vs ΔP) exists for
        IMM/ELO cells; MicroLink's "2 cm bend radius" is unsourced marketing.
        No vibration, acoustic, or shock data. No on-orbit degradation data
        for substrate-free multijunction III-V: the only orbital thin-film
        III-V data point is JAXA's 2009 film-laminated dual-junction
        demonstration, and MicroLink's extensive Zephyr heritage is
        stratospheric — a materially different environment with no
        high-energy particle radiation and no thermal-vacuum cycling.
      </p>

      <h2>Player landscape</h2>
      <table class="nums">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Player</th><th>Demonstrated / claimed</th><th>Scale evidence</th><th>Honest caveat</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {players.map(([p, d, s, c]) => (
            <tr><td>{p}</td><td>{d}</td><td>{s}</td><td>{c}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        Cost anchors: current III-V cells run ~$150/W (Schulte 2023);
        the substrate alone is &gt;$30/W. No public $/W price exists for any
        ELO-based product — MicroLink claims only qualitative "lower
        manufacturing costs." NREL's conditional pathway to $0.40/W requires
        high-volume production <em>and</em> low-cost epitaxy <em>and</em>
        substrate-reuse R&D simultaneously.
      </p>

      <h2>The gaps, as fundable work</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><b>ELO process yield at any scale</b> — the single largest
        evidence gap; no public number exists.</li>
        <li><b>Per-wafer lift-off time in a real line</b> — only coupon-scale
        lateral etch rates are published; all per-wafer times here are
        derived arithmetic.</li>
        <li><b>Demonstrated reuse with per-cycle device data</b> — a
        published "N reuses at constant AM0 efficiency" curve, most valuable
        for Ge after classic ELO (currently undemonstrated).</li>
        <li><b>Space qualification of substrate-free product</b> — an AIAA
        S-111/ECSS-class campaign for a lifted cell or laminate, including
        the missing cyclic-flexure and thermal-vacuum datasets.</li>
        <li><b>ELO product $/W</b> — any verified price or cost breakdown
        for a shipped ELO cell.</li>
        <li><b>MicroLink space-qualification follow-through</b> — the company
        announced AIAA S-111/112 work in 2021; no completion evidence found.
        Watch item, alongside Rocket Lab IMM process disclosures.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>What this changes</h2>
      <p>
        R3 stays on the board, but reframed: the question is no longer "can
        the wafer be reused ~10 times" but "can per-cycle reconditioning cost
        and per-wafer throughput fall far enough that reuse beats both virgin
        Ge and AZUR-style Ge recycling." The substrate-liberation physics is
        real and flying (stratospherically); the economics are unbuilt, and
        the volume player in the market (Rocket Lab/SolAero) has voted with
        its process: substrate removal without reuse. The most decision-
        relevant single artifact would be MicroLink's actual per-wafer
        economics — not public — followed by a demonstrated multi-cycle Ge
        reuse series with device data, which any lab could produce.
      </p>

      <h2>Perimeter and provenance</h2>
      <p>
        Four parallel literature sweeps, 32 sources, admitted to the Surya
        epistemic graph as event <code>ev-000000000010</code> with assessment
        <code>surya-assessment-r3-gap-map-2026-08</code>, judgment
        <b>established-with-gap</b>. Flagged honestly: the two most-cited
        reuse papers (Bauhuis 2010, Adams 2013) are paywalled and their cycle
        counts are known only through secondary paraphrase; the Tampere
        dissertation full text is bot-walled (20 mm/h figure from the
        abstract); conflicting "fastest etch rate" claims exist across
        sources under non-comparable conditions; the JAXA qualification test
        matrix (temperatures, cycle counts) could not be extracted from the
        proceedings PDF; per-wafer times and constellation throughput are
        derived arithmetic, labeled as such; MicroLink scale figures are
        company claims throughout, with only the 37.75% AM1.5G efficiency
        independently certified.
      </p>

      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <ul class="srcs">
        {sources.map(([label, note, url]) => (
          <li><a href={url}>{label}</a> — {note}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
